 it does reduce the cost of analysis  by minimizing the time of analysis  obviously and the use of all the  resources that go into analysis  so that's another  another huge time saver and another  thing that makes your lab faster and and  more nimble  it is compatible with all auto samplers  no matter what auto sampler you're using  you can use  the avs six and seven  so you don't need your own special auto  sampler you don't need our  special auto sampler  so  so that pretty much puts to bed the the  speed uh portion of of the discussion  the uh  the the the three ways that i i said we  would we would make your analysis faster  um  now in terms of ease of use  um  making your life easier intellectual 2.0  this is another facet of intelliquant  that i wanted to discuss along with emmf  and ocf  and we at agilent are very very proud of  our three-letter acronyms so i thought  i'd throw some in for you but i will  tell you what they mean  um  so intelliquat 2.0  is just the second generation of  intelliquant um those of you familiar  with our  uh 5100 and 5110 icp oes systems  um from a few years back uh will  remember  initially in telequan it's a terrific  idea it's a semi-quantitative look at  basically the whole periodic table  and teleport 2.0 takes that  and and kind of takes it to the next  level  by  by actually  giving you a star rating system  when it looks at wavelengths you can  scan your your samples  it'll tell you what's in your samples  and it'll tell you what wavelengths it  thinks you should use to analyze your  samples and which ones it thinks you  shouldn't use  so in this case you've got a star rating  system from one to five stars for all of  these uh emission lines um and then  some of them have a question mark and  those question marks indicate problems  with those specific emission lines uh in  this case you see this boron line is  flagged for uh  having very poor confidence and it's got  a big iron interference so they don't  they they recommend that you don't use  it  and in fact they tell you which one they  they do recommend with the green check  mark  so  it's it's like having a spectroscopist  who's got years of of uh  of uh experience and insight  right there with you doing your method  development  and i realized that 200.7 there's not a  lot of method development that goes in  but there's some  i've set up 2.7 methods at many many  commercial environmental labs  and there's always some level of method  development that goes into this and this  would tend to make it a little bit  easier  granted you don't know what samples are  going to come in the door  but this will certainly  make things  easier when you're developing methods  and when you're running a new sample  type that comes in the door  so um in addition to that the this  simplified list of uh  of wavelengths the ones that actually do  have green check marks and were used uh  by intelliquant uh can be copied and  pasted into a new uh worksheet where you  can just take this information directly  make a new worksheet and that is your  those are your your analyte wavelength  for  per analysis  so  there's intel  2.0 let's go to emf  um  emf uh love the x  acronyms stands for early maintenance  feedback  um early maintenance feedback is really  a customizable maintenance  schedule  uh for your system  you're able to  dial all these  schedules up based on your  specific experience running the  instrument based on your experience with  your samples because nobody knows your  samples better than you  it allows you to  create new maintenance items it allows  you to  customize the existing maintenance items  you can actually have optional pop-up  alerts  that will actually pop up in the middle  of your analysis and tell you that  something needs to be addressed  look at the axial viewing window change  the tubing  clean the nebulizer clean the torch etc  etc  all those things  are able to be turned into a pop-up that  will at least give the analyst cause to  either dismiss or reset those uh those  items um at the instrument  so um in addition uh you can actually  time the uh  the life cycle of each of these items by  the the number of hours the instruments  actually on the number of plasma hours  you run uh the number of samples or just  the sheer number of days  since the last maintenance item has gone  through so uh pretty flexible  and really really handy  especially when you have a lot of users  using an instrument  i think it's extremely handy to have  something like this  in your corner  so uh ocf  uh yet another three-letter  acronym  which stands for  layer conditional formatting  outlier conditional formatting is a  is a feature that allows you to actually  have  track 6  qc parameters uh and to track when you  see the failures of this these qc  parameters they're they're customizable  um you know in the case of like  your result concentration percent rsd  you can have it flagged if your percent  rsv for for a result is over five  percent  but when you get to very low values  all your rfds are over five percent  because they're very low and there's a  lot of noise associated with them so you  can actually have that apply only when  it's three times the mdl or some other  multiple times the mdl  so  these are  kind of scalable to what you want to do  with them  but they uh they pop up with nice  colored flags on your data so that you  can see where the failures happen  um and in the case where you have just a  gigantic amount of data which you which  i frequently do um and you want to be  able to find those failures  it's handy to actually click there's a  there's a show only failures button and  it will just show you the qc uh that  failed  then you're able to dial in and figure  out what's going on with those specific  samples for those specific analytes  which is a little bit easier than trying  to find a needle in a haystack looking  at you know  10 different wavelengths for everything  which is kind of what i do a lot of the  time so  it makes it makes it very simple  um  so those are the the three ease of use  items i wanted to go over intelliquat  2.0  and the ability to uh  to  have the instrument select wavelength  for you  um emf  um so that early maintenance uh  so that you don't do maintenance more  frequently than you need to but you get  the maintenance done in a timely fashion  and it lets you know when the  maintenance is due  and ocf outlier conditional formatting  where you're actually able to  set up your  your flags so that they intelligently  flag your samples and  you're actually able to winnow through  all the data and just find the failures  so that you can address them  and rerun  analysis if need be  so moving on to the accuracy part of the  equation  um  the  the accuracy part as you may have  guessed from my previous slides will  probably have three parts  improved resolution um improve detection  limits and improve stability  so um  i was very impressed first of all with  with our past generations of icp oes  i've been doing icp for upwards of 30  years  and the these these last two generations  the 5100 and the 5110 i thought were  just amazing instruments and i was  absolutely tickled with the performance  and so when when the engineers came um  and talked to us about this new  generation of systems and they said well  the optics are going to be smaller i  thought  oh what did we give up what what what's  going to happen  what  why would we do this um and so uh when  when they presented  this to us they they started talking  about uh  signal density and and things like that  they showed some diagrams of taken from  the the 5110 our previous generation of  systems um this happens to be the  arsenal arsenic at 188  um so you see the peak and you see the  signal density in the on the  two-dimensional array because everything  takes place on the on the  two-dimensional uh chip  so you can see the uh the photon density  there um this is without free-form  optics  now they added a free-form collimating  mirror to the system and this is what it  did  to the signal density  um  and  it it's amazing the the difference  between  this signal density and this uh  the slope of the spectra is incredible  so much more density so much better  defined the peak is so much better  defined  so the resolution has improved radically  the detection limits have improved  radically just because of this one  single optical component  what is this optical component you ask  well let me just quickly show you  this is a diagram or kind of a mock-up  of our  our optics  the optical tank  the torch would be sitting down here in  the  lower right  as you look at it  so what we see here in the lower left is  the detector  so you can see the ray tracing back to  the  the focusing mirror  back through the the uh prism and the  grating there at the lower center  and then there's the freeform  collimating mirror up in the upper right  bringing it down to the the pre-optics  and the  uh entrance slit  in the lower left  yes  um so  the freeform collimating mirror  what is a freeform  freeform optics  are  not  traditionally surfaced optics they're uh  in fact on a on a nano scale they look  kind of like a pringle and in fact some  people like to call this the pringle um  it looks like a pringled potato chip um  it's not really truly that radical when  you look at it because that is on a  grossly exaggerated scale  but  what this does is eliminates aberrations  in the optical image  because as you know in a in a  spectrometer what you're trying to do is  take light which actually focuses in a  uh  it doesn't it there's there's no flat  focus fields in a in a traditional  optical system  so because there's no flat focus you  have to correct for those  those aberrations that take place on the  edge  of the field of focus and that's what  what a freeform optic does  so not only did we put this in the  spectrometer but we actually came up  with a better way to manufacture and  replicate these free form optics  so that is uh  is really the big idea behind the 5800  5900  it allowed us to shrink the optics uh  box actually  by i believe  i i think somewhat something around half  about 50 smaller the optics box is  honestly  about  i don't know about two feet  maybe a little bit more um  and that's it that's that's how that's  how big the optics  box is it's very small  and so of course the added benefit is  that it purges very quickly uh and it's  easy to maintain uh  the temperature it gets up to  temperature quickly because it is  thermostatic so there's a lot of  benefits to having the smaller optics um  it's  but the the performance is is where it's  at so what does all this  uh  hand wavy updates get us  um  and hopefully this doesn't get in the  way in my life it doesn't um so the  the detection limits that we're seeing  um  for some of the kind of common toxic  elements um arsenic at 1.1 cadmium is  always great uh mercury at 0.3 ppb lead  at 1.1 thallium at 1.1 the percent  reduction from the previous detection  limits which were not shabby  is amazing 70 percent for our sync 70  reduction in the detection limit so  this has made a tremendous difference  in  in the numbers that you're going to get  for your low level  uh metals especially the difficult  metals  that you see in environmental analysis  and in the reliability of the data  especially down low uh when you're  having to run your uh run at your at  your cri or or at the uh at the very  very low level at reporting limit uh  this makes those numbers much more  reliable um granted it's going to push  reporting limits down probably um  but  but that's the business we're all in  right  the instrument can do something cool and  new and people start taking advantage of  it and then we all have to live up to  that for the next 10 years until it all  pushes down again  by a factor of 10. so  at any rate um the  the detection limits and 