 composition um a  couple other things uh to mention uh the  in intellipoint also helps you identify  what kind of sample prep problems may  have taken place um the nice thing i've  seen in intelliquat is  um  you get a chlorine number say for  instance there's  uh hcl uh hydrochloric used in your in  your sample prep or they're supposed to  be  if you don't see chlorine then you don't  have hcl in your samples and that maybe  there's a prep problem that you can  identify immediately  conversely maybe someone did use hcl and  they weren't supposed to  you can identify that as well and you  can nip that in the bud and get your  samples prepped properly  so you don't go through the whole prep  process or the whole prep process and  the analysis process and then realize  that they were prepped incorrectly  so there is that  there is also the  argon emission monitor which i find  pretty useful it really just tracks the  emission of  a certain suite of argon lines  and allows you to look at them for each  sample that you ran  so this sort of allows you to track the  plasma efficiency  how hot the plasma was  whether or not there was any issue with  sample interruption sample flow  interruption  or anything like that along the way  or if there was some sort of massive uh  suppression because of sample matrix you  can certainly see the  arsenic  emission  hit the floor  occasionally it will do that as well so  there are  uh ways to uh  there this is a very useful technique to  be able to use uh things like that um  so  moving on from  ways we can  help you make your analysis faster  easier and more accurate  there's sort of what's coming up on the  horizon for environmental  environmental topics  the epa is working actively on ucmr5  and that is  a list of emerging contaminants um that  are likely to be added to  permits in the in the future they're at  least going to be studied um and so that  process is going on right now  uh i think it was a little bit delayed  by uh the covid19 uh issue in 2020  uh  it was a great year wasn't it um  so at this point  it's actually uh in pre-publication  um it's under review uh as of the 20th  of uh last month um once it's published  the epa will host a couple of  identical webinars  and they'll discuss the lab approval  process for this  for the for the pilot study  um  laboratory methods and monitoring  requirements um so all this will be  discussed so this whole process is uh  is kind of  um  well it's something that the epa goes  through about every five years  where they add contaminants in this case  a lot of the contaminants that are being  added are pfas  uh they've been a lot in the news those  uh these these plastics that just don't  go away in our environment um but the  one that they've added for  uh of interest to 200.7 and then those  two run 200.7 is lithium um so if you  have not  or are not looking at lithium uh might  be a good idea to start including that  into your uh into your program um it's  something that is going to be coming up  before long uh and if your laboratory  would like to get on the cutting edge  and uh and maybe help the epa collect  some data then that is certainly  something you can do  uh  there's a whole landing page for ucmr5  uh at epa.gov  you can get involved and you can apply  to be part of the  of the pro proficiency uh  monitoring process so um  i think that's all that i had for now uh  thank you very much for your attention i  appreciate it and uh i look forward to  talking to you again next time  and uh thank you for your participation  and i will be around for questions  during the live virtual environmental  summit thank you  bye
 

